// `std:utf8` — the boundary between VL's strings and the byte world.
//
// A VL `string` IS its UTF-8 bytes: `s.length` is a byte count and `s[i]` is a byte
// (`docs/guide/strings-design.md` §Storage/§API; code points live on `for cp in s` and
// `s.cpAt(i)`). A `u8[]` is bytes too — `u8` is a STORAGE type, so an element reads out
// widened to `i32` (0..255) and truncates on write. The two sides no longer differ in
// STORAGE, so what is left here is the part that never was storage: VALIDATION, POSITIONED
// ERROR REPORTING AND SANITIZATION. §Validity is deliberately Go-lean — the core does no
// validation at the host boundary and lets malformed bytes read as U+FFFD — so nothing in
// the core will ever do those three. The module's audit is `docs/internals/utf8-byte-ready.md`.
//
// It is its own module rather than private to `std:fs` because `std:fs` is merely the FIRST
// consumer: every boundary VL grows (files, paths, argv, stdin) is bytes, and a private copy
// is how a program ends up with two decoders holding two opinions about invalid input.
//
// THE INVALID-INPUT RULING, both directions, because it is the whole design:
//
//   ENCODE IS TOTAL, AND RAW. `encodeUtf8` cannot fail and never repairs: it hands back the
//   storage bytes unexamined (§Wrap), so a string holding a partial sequence round-trips
//   through `encodeUtf8`/`decodeUtf8` as itself. A caller who wants a well-formed result
//   composes it — `decodeUtf8Lossy(s.bytes())` — and pays visibly. A non-scalar cannot reach
//   here at all: the core substitutes U+FFFD in `fromCodePoints` (§NonScalar), so this
//   module keeps no substitution branch that could drift from that one.
//
//   DECODE IS FALLIBLE, AND STRICT BY DEFAULT. `decodeUtf8` returns `string | Utf8Error` and
//   rejects every ill-formed sequence: bad lead byte, bad continuation, overlong form,
//   surrogate, beyond U+10FFFF, truncated tail. That is Rust's `from_utf8`, not
//   `from_utf8_lossy`, and strict is the DEFAULT because a decode failure is information:
//   those bytes are not text, and the program about to treat them as text is about to be
//   wrong. Substituting silently would turn "this is a PNG" into question marks that compare
//   unequal to everything and explain nothing. `decodeUtf8Lossy` is the deliberate opt-out
//   for a caller who would rather render mojibake than stop — a log tailer, an editor — and
//   substitutes one U+FFFD per maximal ill-formed subpart, which is the WHATWG rule.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO. No UTF-16, no other encodings, no BOM handling (a leading
// U+FEFF decodes to U+FEFF and stays in the string — stripping it is a policy for whoever
// knows the format), no normalization, no grapheme or display-width analysis. Those are not
// "later here"; they are a different module.

// `toString` is `std:fmt`'s export, not the ambient compiler builtin it once was (owner
// ruling, DECISIONS.md), so the two digit renderings in `decodeCore`'s error message make
// `std:fmt` a module dependency of this one.
//
// A private decimal renderer here — and another in `std:fs`, and another in `std:args` — is
// REFUSED for the reason `std:fmt`'s header gives about `split`: one implementation beats
// smaller output, and three copies that must agree about i32 min is exactly the drift that
// ruling protects against. VL has no cross-module dead-code elimination, so an unoptimized
// module carries all of `std:str` and `std:fmt` for one i32 rendering; binaryen's DCE at
// `-O3` removes almost all of it. ONE copy is knowingly kept: `std:test`'s `vltI64Str`, so
// the assertion surface stays dependency-free and a fmt defect cannot corrupt its own
// failure report; `tests/cases/std/renderers-agree.vl` pins the two at i64 min.
import { toString } from "std:fmt"

// A decode failure, carrying the position AND the offending byte.
//
// `at` is a BYTE OFFSET — ruled, see `docs/internals/utf8-byte-ready.md` §at — and RELATIVE
// to the `off` passed in, never an absolute index, so it stays meaningful to a caller
// decoding a slice out of a larger array, exactly as Rust's `Utf8Error::valid_up_to()` is.
// `s.slice(0, e.at)` is therefore the valid prefix with no converter, and — decode being a
// byte identity — `at` is at the same time the byte length of that prefix
// (`tests/cases/std/utf8-invariant.vl` §6). `byte` is the lead byte that failed to decode.
//
// `byte` ALSO KEEPS THIS TYPE DISTINCT: VL aliases are STRUCTURAL, so the `{ at, msg }` shape
// `error-handling-design.md` §90 blesses as `ParseError` is literally this type, and a union
// naming both would fail at emit with no recorded members.
export type Utf8Error = { at: i32, byte: i32, msg: string }

// U+FFFD, substituted in both directions.
const REPLACEMENT = 0xFFFD

// THE ENCODE SIDE'S NON-SCALAR RULING LIVES IN THE CORE, NOT HERE. A string can no longer
// hold a lone surrogate or a value past U+10FFFF: `fromCodePoints` substitutes U+FFFD, and
// `print` streams the stored replacement bytes rather than dropping them
// (`docs/internals/utf8-byte-ready.md` §NonScalar). `scalar`/`utf8Width` were deleted rather
// than kept as defensive documentation of that rule, because a second copy of a rule is a
// thing that can disagree with the first, and dead code cannot be caught disagreeing.

// `s.utf8Length()` — the exact length of `s.encodeUtf8()`. A string IS its UTF-8 bytes, so
// this is `self.length` and O(1) (`docs/internals/utf8-byte-ready.md` §utf8Length).
//
// The collapse is sound only because §Wrap ruled `encodeUtf8` RAW: a string holding
// malformed bytes — legal, since slicing off a character boundary is permitted (§Validity) —
// keeps those bytes through `encodeUtf8`, so `.length` counts exactly what the encoder
// emits. Decoding to code points and re-measuring each would instead answer 3 per
// replacement character and disagree with this module's own encoder.
export function utf8Length(self: string) { self.length }

// `s.encodeUtf8()` — `s` as UTF-8 bytes. A string is already its UTF-8 bytes, so this is
// `self.bytes()`: one `array.copy`, at memcpy speed.
//
// RAW, not sanitized (`docs/internals/utf8-byte-ready.md` §Wrap): `bytes()` hands back the
// storage bytes unexamined, so a string holding a partial sequence round-trips through
// `encodeUtf8`/`decodeUtf8` as itself rather than being silently repaired. A caller who
// wants the guarantee composes it — `decodeUtf8Lossy(s.bytes())` — and pays visibly.
//
// FRESH, not a view (§Ownership), so the contract is unchanged: `s.bytes()` COPIES, because
// a `u8[]` wrapper has no `start` field and cannot express a view of a sliced string, and
// because a `u8[]` is MUTABLE — an aliasing `bytes()` would hand out a writable pointer into
// an immutable string's storage. The result is the fresh, growable array callers always got.
export function encodeUtf8(self: string): u8[] { return self.bytes() }

// The one decode loop. The strict/lossy fork is a PARAMETER rather than two copies, so the
// two public spellings cannot disagree about what "ill-formed" means.
//
// The validity table is the standard one; every row rejects a sequence a naive shift-and-or
// would decode. Accepting one is the classic security bug — two byte strings decoding to one
// string is how a path check that already ran gets walked past.
//   C0 C1        overlong two-byte forms of ASCII
//   E0 80..9F    overlong three-byte forms
//   ED A0..BF    the surrogate block
//   F0 80..8F    overlong four-byte forms
//   F4 90..BF    beyond U+10FFFF
//   F5..FF       no such lead byte
function decodeCore(
  self: u8[],
  off: i32,
  len: i32,
  lossy: boolean,
): string | Utf8Error {
  const cps: i32[] = []
  let i = 0
  while i < len {
    const b0 = self[off + i]
    // The sequence length comes from the lead byte, together with the TIGHTENED
    // bounds the FIRST continuation byte must satisfy (every later one is the
    // plain 0x80..0xBF).
    let size = 0
    let cp = 0
    let lo = 0x80
    let hi = 0xBF
    if b0 < 0x80 {
      size = 1
      cp = b0
    } else if b0 >= 0xC2 && b0 <= 0xDF {
      size = 2
      cp = b0 & 0x1F
    } else if b0 >= 0xE0 && b0 <= 0xEF {
      size = 3
      cp = b0 & 0x0F
      if b0 == 0xE0 { lo = 0xA0 }
      if b0 == 0xED { hi = 0x9F }
    } else if b0 >= 0xF0 && b0 <= 0xF4 {
      size = 4
      cp = b0 & 0x07
      if b0 == 0xF0 { lo = 0x90 }
      if b0 == 0xF4 { hi = 0x8F }
    }
    // `good` counts the MAXIMAL VALID SUBPART — how far the sequence got before
    // it went wrong, never less than 1. It is what the lossy path advances by,
    // so a truncated 4-byte sequence at the end of the range yields ONE U+FFFD
    // rather than one per leftover byte. That is the WHATWG rule, and it is also
    // the only advance that cannot loop: `good >= 1` always.
    let ok = size > 0
    let good = 1
    let k = 1
    while ok && k < size {
      if i + k >= len {
        ok = false
      } else {
        const b = self[off + i + k]
        let blo = 0x80
        let bhi = 0xBF
        if k == 1 {
          blo = lo
          bhi = hi
        }
        if b < blo || b > bhi {
          ok = false
        } else {
          cp = (cp << 6) | (b & 0x3F)
          good = good + 1
        }
      }
      k = k + 1
    }
    if ok {
      cps.push(cp)
      i = i + size
    } else if lossy {
      cps.push(REPLACEMENT)
      i = i + good
    } else {
      return {
        at: i,
        byte: b0,
        msg: "invalid UTF-8: byte " + toString(b0) + " at offset " + toString(i),
      }
    }
  }
  fromCodePoints(cps)
}

// `b.decodeUtf8()` — the whole array as a string, or the position of the first
// ill-formed sequence. STRICT; see the header for why strict is the default.
export function decodeUtf8(self: u8[]): string | Utf8Error {
  decodeCore(self, 0, self.length, false)
}

// `b.decodeUtf8At(off, len)` — the same, over one range. The range form exists
// because a consumer that splits a byte block into records (`std:fs`'s
// directory listing, a line reader) must attribute a decode failure to the
// RECORD, not to the block.
export function decodeUtf8At(
  self: u8[],
  off: i32,
  len: i32,
): string | Utf8Error {
  decodeCore(self, off, len, false)
}

// `b.decodeUtf8Lossy()` — the whole array, with one U+FFFD per maximal ill-formed subpart.
// Total: there is no input this rejects.
//
// IT IS A SANITIZER, NOT A DECODER, and that is why it does not collapse into the core's
// lenient wrap. §Validity is Go-lean: a string is bytes that are *usually* UTF-8, so the core
// will wrap ill-formed bytes and let them read as U+FFFD only when something iterates them —
// and that wrap KEEPS THE ORIGINAL BYTES, which `s.bytes()` hands back unchanged and
// `s.length` counts. This function REWRITES them: what comes out is guaranteed well-formed
// UTF-8, whose bytes are EF BF BD where the bad bytes were, and the original is gone. Go
// draws the same line between `string(b)` and `strings.ToValidUTF8`. A caller who wants
// "show me something" wants the wrap; one who wants "bytes I can hand onward as text" wants
// this.
export function decodeUtf8Lossy(self: u8[]) {
  const r = decodeCore(self, 0, self.length, true)
  // Unreachable: the lossy path substitutes rather than returning the error arm.
  // The narrowing is what makes the `string` return type provable, and the trap
  // is what makes a future edit that breaks the invariant loud instead of
  // silent — VL has no way to say "this arm cannot happen" in a type.
  if r is Utf8Error {
    __trap__("std:utf8: lossy decode reported an error")
    return ""
  }
  r
}
